%I A397769 #26 Aug 26 2026 18:29:53
%S A397769 1,3,27,3623878683
%N A397769 a(1) = 1; a(n+1) = a(n) * (2^a(n) + 1).
%C A397769 a(5) contains 1090896194 digits and is too large to include.
%C A397769 This sequence arises in the card game Magic: The Gathering. You start with a card called Doubling Season, which doubles all token copies you create. When you make a token copy of all Doubling Seasons you control on the battlefield simultaneously, the total number of Doubling Seasons you have afterwards is the next term.
%C A397769 A034797 is the sequence you get if you make a copy of only one Doubling Season at each step instead of all of them.
%H A397769 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magic%3A_The_Gathering">Magic: The Gathering</a>.
%H A397769 Wizards of the Coast, <a href="https://gatherer.wizards.com/RAV/en-us/158/doubling-season">Doubling Season</a>.
%H A397769 Wizards of the Coast, <a href="https://magic.wizards.com/en/rules">Rules of Magic: The Gathering</a>.
%e A397769 a(2) = 1 + 1*2^1 = 3.
%o A397769 (Python)
%o A397769 def A(n):
%o A397769     a = 1
%o A397769     for _ in range(n):
%o A397769         a = a * (1 + 2**a)
%o A397769     return a
%o A397769 print([A(n) for n in range(4)])
%Y A397769 Cf. A034797.
%K A397769 nonn,new
%O A397769 1,2
%A A397769 _Joshua B. Weinstein_, Aug 13 2026
